Subject: vulkan: don't request VK_KHR_shader_bfloat16 the driver doesn't expose

ggml-vulkan derives two independent bf16 facts and conflates them at device
creation:

  * `bfloat16_support` — VK_KHR_shader_bfloat16 is in the physical device's
    extension list (ggml-vulkan.cpp, the extension-enumeration loop).
  * `device->coopmat_bf16_support` — VK_KHR_cooperative_matrix reports a
    BF16 x BF16 -> F32 subgroup configuration.

The second is set from cooperative-matrix properties alone, and then pushes
"VK_KHR_shader_bfloat16" into the device extension list on its own. A driver
that reports BF16 matrix configurations without exposing the bfloat16 shader
extension therefore fails `vkCreateDevice` outright:

    loader_validate_device_extensions: Device extension VK_KHR_shader_bfloat16
        not supported by selected physical device or enabled layers.
    vkCreateDevice: Failed to validate extensions in list
    vk::PhysicalDevice::createDevice: ErrorExtensionNotPresent

Every Vulkan lane on such a machine is unusable — and the failure is not
survivable in-process: the caught exception unwinds into ggml's teardown of a
half-initialized device, and the Vulkan loader aborts the process (0xC0000409)
when `vkDestroyFence` is called with a device that was never created.

Observed on an AMD Radeon 760M (RDNA3 iGPU), driver 32.0.13046.7002 (May 2025),
Windows 11 — the device banner states the contradiction plainly:

    ggml_vulkan: 0 = AMD Radeon 760M Graphics (AMD proprietary driver) | uma: 1
        | fp16: 1 | bf16: 0 | ... | matrix cores: KHR_coopmat

The capability, not just the extension request, is what has to be gated: only
suppressing the `push_back` would leave `coopmat_bf16_support` set and build
bf16 cooperative-matrix shaders against an extension that was never enabled.
So this clears the flag next to the existing GGML_VK_DISABLE_BFLOAT16 opt-out,
which is the same suppression by a different trigger.

Machines that do expose VK_KHR_shader_bfloat16 keep bf16 cooperative matrices
and are bit-for-bit unaffected, so this changes no published measurement.

Verified — RTX 5080 + RDNA2 iGPU, Linux, `-DGGML_VULKAN=ON`. glslc there reports
COOPMAT / COOPMAT2 / BFLOAT16 support, so the `#if` region this sits in is
actually compiled. With ggml fault-injected into the 760M's believed state (the
extension hidden from its detection loop, a BF16 coopmat configuration
reported), the extension list handed to `vkCreateDevice`:

    unpatched   12 extensions, includes VK_KHR_shader_bfloat16   <- while bf16: 0
    patched     11 extensions, excludes it                       <- same bf16: 0

A device that genuinely has the extension is untouched: `probe` stays valid,
`bf16: 1`, same list.

Not reproduced end-to-end off the affected hardware: the loader only rejects
when the *driver* lacks the extension, and neither local GPU can be that device
(the 5080 has it for real; the RDNA2 iGPU reports no cooperative matrix at all,
so it never sets the flag). On the 760M itself, `GGML_VK_DISABLE_BFLOAT16=1` —
the identical assignment three lines above this gate — takes `bench check` from
"`probe` produced no valid events" to a full pass (probe 1.7 TFLOP/s on
vulkan:0, sweep and run healthy). This patch is that behaviour without the
contributor having to know the env var exists.

Upstream status: present on master as of 2026-07-29 (same unguarded push_back).
Not submitted upstream — llama.cpp's AGENTS.md does not accept
predominantly AI-generated pull requests, and this patch was written by an
agent. Drop this file and the patches/ wiring in CMakeLists.txt if the pin
moves to a commit that fixes it.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp b/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp
index e7d04634b..0d3375780 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp
@@ -6065,6 +6065,13 @@ static vk_device ggml_vk_get_device(size_t idx) {
             if (getenv("GGML_VK_DISABLE_BFLOAT16")) {
                 device->coopmat_bf16_support = false;
             }
+            // A driver can report BF16 cooperative-matrix configurations without
+            // exposing VK_KHR_shader_bfloat16 itself. Leaving the flag set pushes
+            // that extension into vkCreateDevice below, which then fails the whole
+            // device with VK_ERROR_EXTENSION_NOT_PRESENT.
+            if (!bfloat16_support) {
+                device->coopmat_bf16_support = false;
+            }
         }

         if (device->coopmat_support) {
